ps 146; on the six watched boards 58 and 8). (a) Which countries are 'English-speaking': the default is the narrow list, the United States, United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland. NOT included, with what each would bring back: India 20 postings (1 on the six boards), Singapore 22 (0), Philippines 3, Hong Kong 3, South Africa 0. (b) A location that names no country ('Remote', 'Hybrid', 'N/A', 'EMEA', 'Remote - Europe') is KEPT (if_no_country_named: keep) and the record says location.country_named is false, because a place the reader cannot read should reach a person, not vanish: 50 postings in the snapshot, 2 of 82 in the 2026-10-18 run. Set it to \"reject\" to drop them. (c) A posting in several places is allowed if ANY one is ('Berlin, Germany | Austin, TX' is kept; a line of only foreign places is rejected; a foreign place beside one that cannot be read is kept). (d) Puerto Rico counts as the United States; 'Georgia' alone counts as the American state. HOW A PLACE IS READ, so it can be done by hand: the line is split on the separators into places; in each place the first of these that is found decides it: a country's own name or abbreviation (names), then a state or province code after a comma, hyphen or bracket ('Athens, GA' is Georgia, USA; codes), then a city or region (places). Names that are also American towns (Lebanon, Jamaica, Holland, Berlin) sit among the places, so a state code beats them. Longer names win ('New Mexico' is not Mexico). A string two countries share goes to the allowed one: a stray posting costs less than a lost one. Words in workplace_words ('Remote', 'Hybrid') are not places. Accents are ignored ('Sao Paulo'). MEASURED: against the country codes the boards themselves report, on 37,470 postings where both the board and the reader name a country, they agree on 37,458; the 12 that differ are postings in several places (the board reports one code) and the country Georgia. KNOWN WEAK SPOTS: a foreign city with no country 